From 0b922bc80f7a1a93f39bdfea85909f885ba9a787 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 11:21:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 06/11] [Perf][Model][Qwen4Exp] Opt-in int8 quantization of the
 pipeline handoff

The hyper-connection model ships the materialized 4-stream state
(hidden*hc_count bf16) across every stage hop: 42 MB per 2048-token
chunk on this family. On CMP 170HX PCIe gen2 (~1.66 GB/s per pair
measured) the wire is the prefill wall: the first profiled 1M-token
prefill spends 35.6% of device time in nccl kernels whose 26.7 ms mean
equals that payload over the link rate.

VLLM_QWEN4EXP_PP_INT8_HOP=1 puts per-token, per-stream symmetric int8
plus fp32 scales on the wire instead. Live measurement: cold 1M TTFT
91.8 -> 79.9 s (x3 fresh salts), 100/4 wall 386 -> 346 s (x2), warm
TTFT, decode rate, acceptance (4.79-4.86) and prefix-state needles
unchanged; pool +12k from halved receive buffers. Flag-off is
byte-identical to the pre-patch path on eager and CUDA-graph boots.

Receiving stages raise instead of silently casting when a plain bf16
handoff arrives with the flag set, so a half-enabled pipeline cannot
corrupt values.

Also unbind upstream's instance-attribute factory wiring in __init__:
it silently shadows this class method, and a bf16 dummy handoff from
profile_run hit the strict half-enable guard on the 2026-10-27 boot. A
test pins both arms of the method and forbids the factory binding from
returning unnoticed at the next rebase.
